# rm301, the listening brim (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm301
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent broad light
tier standard
written_for rm_gwen_fogstack
written_by codex
title the listening brim
fits stage=adult|old
prefers job=merchant drive=restless
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=restless
quirk turns_one_corner_of_an_oilcloth_brim_up_to_hear_a_float
likes smoked_eel evening_line quiet_chapel frosty_mornings
dislikes salt_wind flies
fears a_bite_lost_in_the_wind
hopes a_quiet_cast_before_supper
topics pastimes +0.6 weather +0.4 home +0.2 travel +0.3
styles comfort=company gossip=plain refuse=level tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# turns one corner of an oilcloth brim up for sound and the other down for shelter, always alert for a fishing float rattling against a bank.

words
oath! = Holes in my brim | Seven soaked floats
saying! = Keep one ear out of cover | Still water has little knocks
hood = my broad oilcloth | the old hood
brim = the stiff front fold | a corner of the brim
earfold = a turned-up corner | the shallow ear fold
float = my little cork | the yellow float
linecord = the fishing cord | my evening line
knock = a light knock | the little cork noise
pin = a horn pin | the fastening peg
bank = the bank edge | the low shore
wrinkle = a sharp crease | an old wrinkle
roofflap = the loose edge | a strip of oilcloth
strip = a length of smoked eel | the dark fish slice
reed = a damp reed | the bent stem

any
opinion @1 houses against 1 : From below, a fine high roof looks chiefly remarkable for how far its rain can fall.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : An oily shine is scarcely proof a hood will keep the neck dry.
opinion @1 sump for 1 [self.quarter=sump] : A bit of cloth has a long useful life down our way. A second crease won't kill it.
opinion @1 church for 1 : A chapel's quiet shuts a fair portion of the weather outside a wolf as well as outside the roof.
opinion @1 road for 1 : Bring the hood in case then follow the dry patches till the afternoon runs out.
opinion @1 watch against 1 : A quiet watcher at a shop door can see more than a whole march of loud boots.

grown
line @1 greet : Hello. I had the listening side turned your way. | Good. A voice instead of a drip.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : First time I've caught that footfall, I think. | Welcome. The open fold carries your words well.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Fresh air under the fold at last. | My ears have had their first stretch of the day.
line @1 greet.return : Yes, I heard you knocking on the air. | Hello in return. I'm wide awake under here.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Good sound. I've heard that voice in all weathers. | I'm glad I turned the brim up for you.
line @3 greet.return [here.time=night] : Late greetings. Easier to hear footsteps when the light's gone. | Hello there across the dark.
line @1 again.open : You're back in range of my better ear. | Another little knock from a familiar direction.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Come over to the dry edge and listen. | Stand here. There's a small sound worth catching.
line @1 farewell : May your hood fit the weather you find. | Go enjoy the view past this bit of shelter.
line @1 farewell.return : I've a last listen to take before leaving too. | Away you go, well beyond the drips.
line @1 react.news : Let me turn this corner up and hear properly. | I'll give the whole account a quiet ear.
line @1 react.news.new : That hadn't made so much as a ripple round me. | Entirely new hearing under this hood.
line @1 react.news.known : I caught a fair version of that earlier. | Yes, that bit sounded the same on its first journey.
line @1 react.news.doubt : I'd listen for another sound before calling that a bite. | There's an echo in there I'm unwilling to trust.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|crime|assault|loss|theft] : Cruel news. I'll stop fussing with my things and sit. | You can speak quietly about that here. I'll hear you.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|work] : Ah, that's better than a sound fish taking the float. | Good news deserves both ears uncovered.
line @1 ask.how : Did you find a little peace anywhere today? | How's life sounding from your corner of the shelter?
line @1 answer.how : Restless, though I've enough sense to rest when my feet insist. | Doing well enough for an evening's cast.
line @1 decline : I'd be leaving something important quite unattended if I did that. | I haven't a free evening to promise for it.
line @1 accept_decline : That's settled. I'll leave you your own choice. | Fair enough. I'll cast elsewhere.
line @1 preclose : My ears are still interested but my legs want a walk. | I'll move along while there's a little dry ground.
line @1 accept_preclose : Off with you. Sitting forever would hardly suit either of us. | Go on to your next good prospect.
line @1 thank : I'll remember you held that corner for me. | You've made a wet little business a pleasure. Thanks.
line @1 deflect_thanks : One bit of dry cover doesn't cost much to share. | I'd already lifted the brim and seen what was needed.
line @1 sympathise : I've room under this bit of cover for some quiet. | Sit near me. Words needn't come straight away.
line @1 reassure : Turn just one corner up. Sometimes that's view enough. | There'll be an interval between the heavy drops.
line @1 ack : I'm hearing the distinction you mean. | That came across clean through the wind.
line @1 give.advice : Fasten the hood where you can undo it with one paw. | Don't let the wind take both corners at once.
scene foldodd
when topic=pastimes self.likes=evening_line
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I pin one corner of my hood up when my float goes onto the water. | The brim looks uneven because I want it that way.
b : Aren't you letting rain straight in? | Which corner goes up?
a : One ear can stand a few drops if it means hearing the float. | The one facing the water. I'd hear a rattle from the cork if it catches against the bank.

scene fly_rim
when topic=animals self.dislikes=flies
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A fly under a close hood can make itself quite a public nuisance. | The smallest winged thing picks the largest possible echo inside this brim.
b : Take the hood off then? | Does turning the edge help?
a : I do. It tends to leave at the first sign of an open audience. | Enough for an exit. I can wait for my peace to come back.

scene dampnotch
when topic=weather here.weather=rain
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Every crease in #hood# develops a separate drip point. | A hood with one corner up drains most agreeably off the other one.
b : Is the odd shape intentional? | And a shower driven sideways?
a : Partly. An ear out, a shoulder wet, the neck dry. Acceptable business. | I turn. Rain has no strong claim on the direction of my nose.

scene highwind
when topic=weather self.dislikes=salt_wind
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : A salt wind makes the edge of my hood as stiff as old fish skin. | I don't begrudge the wind its salt till it rubs into my ears.
b : Have to wash the cloth? | Is a little salt so troublesome?
a : A wipe with fresh water makes a new thing of the fold. | On a plate it's excellent. Between a fold and a moving ear it's abominable.

scene frostcork
when topic=weather self.likes=frosty_mornings
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I like lifting a float from its little ring of frost. | A cold white rim shows exactly where a cork rested overnight.
b : Does it stay white beneath? | Can the cork freeze fast?
a : No. There's a brown circle in the white, accurate as a drawn mark. | In a wet pot, yes. I set mine on a dry board so the lift is clean.

scene quiet_swim
when topic=pastimes self.likes=evening_line
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Evening makes the little noises of a casting line plain. | I take more pleasure in hearing the first cast than counting the catch.
b : A cast doesn't sound like much. | Wouldn't you rather eat the fish?
a : A brief whistle and a quiet plop. That's quite enough noise for the occasion. | Certainly, if there's a fish to eat. The cast I'll have either way.

scene smoke_eel
when topic=food self.likes=smoked_eel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Smoke on eel means the fish can wait a little longer for dinner. | I like an evening meal I can carry on a line-watching walk.
b : You'd eat what you're trying to catch? | What travels that well?
a : Smoked eel in my pocket, live fish in the water. They occupy perfectly different places. | A folded strip of smoked fish. Better than a bowl under a flapping hood.

scene quietchapel
when topic=faith self.likes=quiet_chapel
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : In a quiet chapel I uncover both ears and let them do nothing for a while. | The chapel can have my whole attention if it leaves the rain outdoors.
b : Do you listen for anything particular? | Where does the hood go?
a : No. It's pleasant to have both ears ready without needing either one. | Fold it ready for leaving. Then I stop preparing to leave.

scene childvisit
when topic=family self.parent=yes self.child
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd offer #self.child# a nice dry stretch of rail to lean on for a cast. | I'd be pleased to share a whole quiet bank with #self.child#.
b : Would that make for much visiting? | Only one line between you?
a : Excellent visiting. Nothing stops us speaking when we've something to say. | One line leaves one wolf free to see where the cork lands. An advantage.

scene watchdoors
when topic=work self.job=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A door has a way of telling you whether a visitor is coming or simply passing. | I keep the hearing corner open during work just as I would near water.
b : You listen to the hinges? | Does every visitor knock?
a : Footsteps. The beats get closer just before a wolf stops to enter. | No. A little slowing of the step serves much the same purpose.

scene quietkin
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I could share a stretch of quiet water with #self.friend# and count it a visit. | I'd set a second dry place on a bank for #self.friend#.
b : And talk about the catch? | Are you expecting company?
a : If there is one. With company I trust I've no need to invent a subject. | Hoping, perhaps. A dry place will keep till somebody comes.

scene gatehook
when topic=travel here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : You can wait just off the approach to Northgate and sort the carts from the walkers by ear. | Wind catches the light covered carts as they turn through Northgate.
b : What sound does a cart make in wind? | Could you hear it under that hood?
a : A quick flap of canvas between the wheel noises. Like a great loose brim. | On my listening side, yes. I leave the other ear some peace.

scene thinwall
when topic=home self.quarter=sump
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I hang my oilcloth low at home so the dripping doesn't carry through the boards. | In the Sump a little drip can be quite a performance next door.
b : How does hanging it low help? | Are your walls that thin?
a : Set the edge close above a rag. Hardly any fall, hardly any patter. | Thin enough to make me civil about where I put a soaking piece of cloth.

scene hoodprice
when topic=prices
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Show me the inner join before paying for a hood. | Even cheap oilcloth ought to bend without opening a white crack.
b : What does the crack show? | Looks sound on the outside.
a : Where the stiff place has begun to give. Water will explore it soon enough. | Turn the fold against the light. The next shower will be rather less polite.

scene hillsilence
when topic=memories
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I remember a quiet hillside where the smallest sound seemed to travel further than was sensible. | The smell of charred wood can bring a whole hillside back to my nose.
b : What did you hear up there? | A quiet place, was it?
a : A twig knocking on another twig. It would have vanished in a wash of street noise. | Quiet enough for a wolf to hear a bent branch settle back after passing.

scene busytime
when topic=times world.times=lean|hard
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : When times are thin the smallest repair to a hood earns its keep. | A bit of sound thread is an admirable thing to have in a lean week.
b : Would you patch the brim again? | Doesn't a patch leak too?
a : Until the old and new cloth part company quite beyond hope. Then I'll save the pins. | Not if the seam sheds water over the old piece. Direction counts as much as cloth.

scene clearcast
when topic=dreams
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like enough clear evenings to try all the little banks I pass in a hurry. | I've been looking at water and thinking I might give a new spot one quiet cast.
b : Are you hunting a famous catch? | Much to pack for that?
a : Somewhere the cork won't get shoved against the bank by every breeze. A small ambition. | The line, my hood and supper in case the fish are too restless for my line.

scene wordfloat
when topic=gossip
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : Hearing a noise up close is different from knowing what made it. | A flapping bit of cloth can sound very much like a knock if you're expecting company.
b : Would you just ignore it? | You're talking about gossip, aren't you?
a : I'd turn and look. The sound alone proves little more. | Yes. I don't name a visitor till a face has actually come into view.

story turnaround [topic=pastimes|memories]
  : I fished a whole quiet interval with the listening corner lifted. Heard a little knock at precisely regular intervals.
  : Turned my head and it stopped. Turned back and it began again.
  b : Something on your line?
  : The horn pin tapping the hood. The fish had spent that interval as quietly as intended.

story hoodmeal [topic=memories|food]
  : I put a wrapped bit of eel in my hood once to leave my paws free for winding a line.
  : Rain began. Up went the hood with a perfectly executed swing.
  b : Where'd the eel land?
  : In my lap, fortunately. I wouldn't care to explain a smoked fish caught with my head.

story frostprint [topic=weather|memories]
  : I laid my hood on a frosty board with the little folded corner facing upward. The next time I looked there were two white triangles.
  : For a moment I thought I'd acquired a spare oilcloth.
  b : Was one the print of the other?
  : Exactly. An excellent second hood for weather cool enough to let it exist and without a drop of actual rain coming down.

rule @pin
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Let me get this one corner turned up for hearing. | There's the fold settled. Now try that sound again.

rule @cast
when concept=bark self.likes=evening_line
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Cork free of the bank, cord clear of the reeds. | A little room for the float to turn, please.

rule @rain
when concept=bark here.weather=rain
does aside
cooldown 8h
say One crease is sending a stream down the wrong shoulder. | Turn the dripping end where it won't wet somebody else.

rule @ear
when concept=bark
does aside
cooldown 8h
say I heard a little tapping beyond the cloth. | There's a quieter sound behind that first one.

dlg @1 resident.hub : I've the brim up. An ordinary speaking voice will reach me easily.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#, I'd know that step through a whole thickness of oilcloth.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : I've turned my ear where my eyes won't reach. There's still room for talk.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=wary|hostile] : The dry place on the far side will serve for asking questions.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=merchant] : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. A little warning from approaching feet is a great convenience.
dlg @2 resident.job : I'd like a bit of movement in my work and a chance to sit when I've had it.
dlg @1 resident.bye : I hope the wind hits the closed side on your walk.
graft resident.hub
choice How do you get an oilcloth brim to stay up? -> hornfast
node hornfast
say One small pin through the folded edge. Don't bother pinning the whole corner back. A little gap at ear height is enough to let the sound come in.
free
choice Wouldn't rain come through the pinhole? -> smallpin
choice I can see the listening gap. -> hub
node smallpin
say It will if you pierce the crown. That's why my pin goes through two folds of brim. The water that comes through there is on its way down already.
choice I'll keep the crown whole. -> hub
choice Good fishing to you. -> bye
